# THE RIGHT TO SEE THE CONSEQUENCE

## Punitive humanism: when care starts hurting you for your own good

You walk into a shop, pick up a pack of cigarettes, and meet a pair of lungs that clearly failed the audition for a wellness brochure. The state says the warning has been delivered, the manufacturer has complied with the law, and the person looks at the image, pays, walks outside, and lights a cigarette. Bureaucratically, the books appear to balance: the risk is visible, the legal duty has been performed, and the space once occupied by the Marlboro cowboy has been reassigned to mortality.

Yet there is a continent between “the person was warned” and “the person was helped.” What actually happened inside that encounter? Did the person learn something new, understand the risk more clearly, see a path toward another behaviour, or simply receive another dose of fear and then do exactly what they had planned to do? If they smoke anyway, what remains after the image: better knowledge about tobacco, or a harsher judgement about themselves?

This is not an essay arguing that smoking is secretly harmless. Its major health risks are well established, and turning that into a conspiracy of doctors would be intellectual parkour performed over a very shallow pool. Nor is this an essay claiming that pictorial warnings “do not work.” A [large meta-analysis of 37 experimental studies involving 33,613 participants](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25948713/) found that pictorial warnings attract and hold attention better than text-only warnings, produce stronger cognitive and emotional reactions, and increase intentions to avoid smoking or quit.

The question is more demanding: if an intervention works, does that automatically make the way it works humane? If not, where is the boundary between necessary warning, psychological influence, and punishment that has learned to introduce itself as care? At that point the cigarette pack stops being merely a tobacco-control object and becomes a small rectangular mirror for the society holding it.

## The strange shop of the future

Imagine a **strange shop of the future** in which society wakes up one morning and decides to become perfectly consistent in its care. Not kinder and not crueler, merely consistent all the way down, without the familiar human clause that says a principle is beautiful until it reaches our own department. If it is good for adults to be repeatedly confronted with the worst plausible consequence of a legal pleasure, then the rule should travel.

You buy a car, and the doors carry a photograph of a child’s lungs beside a congested road. The dashboard displays the aftermath of a serious collision, large enough to remain part of the driving experience between home and the supermarket. The car is still legal and nobody confiscates the keys; society has simply decided that every pleasure should periodically look back at you through its darkest possible outcome.

The battery of an electric vehicle carries a code leading not to a page with a green leaf and the word “future,” but to the material biography of the battery: extraction, processing, factories, transport, and end of life. For cobalt, that biography can include documented hazardous artisanal mining in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including child labour. [Amnesty International](https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/afr62/7395/2017/en/) has traced how cobalt linked to such conditions could enter supply chains serving major battery, electronics, and automotive companies, and the precision matters: a morally powerful image does not earn permission to be factually careless just because we approve of its lesson.

None of this makes the electric car “bad.” In many contexts it can still have a substantially better environmental profile than a comparable internal-combustion vehicle. What changes is the fairy-tale translation of “zero tailpipe emissions” into “zero consequences.” Mines, electricity, manufacturing, logistics, end-of-life systems, and human labour return to a story from which advertising normally edits them out with admirable efficiency.

You buy a leather jacket, and the label carries not only a brand and a size but a route back to the material before it became fashion. You buy meat, and next to the rosemary, the black tray, and the cow on the label that appears to have personally approved the business model, you can access the product’s earlier life. The point need not be to turn the meat aisle into a horror-film retrospective; it can simply be to stop pretending that a product’s biography begins when the supermarket lights turn on.

You buy a new smartphone, and the box shows not only glass, metal, and this year’s slightly thinner digital messiah, but the mine, the factory, the container ship, energy use, and electronic waste. Every “most advanced phone we have ever made” has one remarkably mystical property: a few years later it becomes “an old phone,” while the planet receives no button marked “delete previous version.” Again, no prohibition is required; the object simply gets its material history back.

You save for three years and finally book a trip to Hawaii. Before payment, the interface displays the approximate climate footprint of the journey and enough context to understand what aviation means inside the wider emissions system, not to ban the ocean but because our newly consistent society has stopped giving favourite pleasures diplomatic immunity. If the principle is that people should repeatedly face the consequences of their enjoyment, why should a dream vacation be exempt from its own causal tail?

The logic would keep travelling. A bottle of wine could carry the image of severe liver disease, a cheeseburger a cardiovascular warning, and a sofa the consequences of a sedentary life. Consumer credit could confront you with a household overwhelmed by debt, while a social platform could open with the face of someone who can no longer look at themselves without a filter. A work laptop could show a burned-out manager writing “just a gentle reminder” at two in the morning while no longer remembering to whom or why. These consequences are not equal in kind or magnitude, and that is precisely why the thought experiment is useful: it does not claim equivalence, it tests the consistency of the communicative principle.

Follow that principle far enough and the humane shop becomes a museum of possible catastrophes. A toothbrush would arrive with a compact course on microplastics, global supply chains, labour conditions, transport emissions, ocean pollution, and your own insufficient moral purity. The cashier would become unnecessary; a pocket-sized Dante with a payment terminal could simply ask whether you have adequately contemplated all nine circles of causality before taking a bag.

The important point is that we do not usually communicate this way, even though other legal products and activities also have consequences. Cars kill, alcohol damages health, manufacturing carries environmental and social costs, and consumer goods do not materialise out of pure marketing and benevolent intention. In most areas we do not consider it normal to force the buyer to look repeatedly at the most horrifying plausible consequence in close-up, which is why the cigarette pack is philosophically interesting as a **moral exception society has granted itself**.

## When information stops being only information

It would be easy to object that the comparison is unfair because a cigarette is not a plane ticket, a car is not a steak, and a smartphone is not a nicotine dependence. That objection is correct, and a serious essay should not flatten different risks into one dramatic collage merely because the rhetoric photographs well. The magnitude of risk, the role of addiction, and the distribution of harm between the user and other people all matter.

But differences in risk do not settle the separate question of **how risk is communicated**. Between the fact that a behaviour is dangerous and the decision to repeatedly expose one group of people to the most severe image associated with that danger lies another layer, populated not only by medicine and statistics but by culture, morality, authority, and our assumptions about who may legitimately be addressed through compulsory disgust. In that layer, risk becomes a design of relationship.

The research gives us a useful clue. A [meta-analysis of 57 studies from 13 countries involving more than 42,000 participants](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32565612/) found that pictorial warnings increased thinking about harm and produced more fear and other negative affect, while having little effect on perceived likelihood or severity of the risk itself. In other words, the person may already know that smoking is dangerous; the image makes the known danger emotionally hotter, nearer, and harder to ignore.

